{
  "facts": [
    {
      "text": "Nintendo guided to 200,000 million yen in consolidated profit attributable to owners of parent for the year ending March 31, 2021 (PDF p. 2). Against the 258.6 billion yen earned in the year ended March 31, 2020 (PDF p. 3), the guide implied a decline of 58.6 billion yen (200.0 minus 258.6, in billions of yen), or 22.7% (58.6 divided by 258.6, rounded).",
      "citations": [
        {
          "passage_id": "P5",
          "quote": "Year ending Mar. 2021 | 1,200,000 | (8.3) | 300,000 | (14.9) | 290,000 | 200,000(19.5)(22.7) | 1,678.93"
        },
        {
          "passage_id": "P3",
          "quote": "profit attributable to owners of parent of 200.0 billion yen."
        },
        {
          "passage_id": "P1",
          "quote": "net profit attributable to owners of parent totaled 258.6\nbillion yen for this fiscal year."
        }
      ],
      "calculation_ids": ["C1", "C2"]
    },
    {
      "text": "Production and shipping delays appeared to be easing, but Nintendo did not say component supply had normalized. It warned that persistent difficulty sourcing necessary parts, or a longer or worse COVID-19 disruption, could constrain product supply (PDF p. 3).",
      "citations": [
        {
          "passage_id": "P2",
          "quote": "It appears that delays in production and shipping are gradually recovering."
        },
        {
          "passage_id": "P2",
          "quote": "issues involving the procurement of necessary components. In addition, if the impact of COVID-19 is prolonged or worsens\nfurther, it may disrupt the product supply."
        }
      ],
      "calculation_ids": []
    }
  ],
  "analysis": {
    "text": "Base case at the results date: within ±2% of the 200,000 million yen guide, inclusive, for the year ending March 31, 2021. The main driver is continued recovery in Switch shipments. If parts arrive in time to build and ship consoles, new owners can buy both existing games and planned releases. That supports the hardware and software sales already contemplated in guidance rather than, by itself, implying a beat (PDF pp. 2–4).",
    "citations": [
      {
        "passage_id": "P5",
        "quote": "Year ending Mar. 2021 | 1,200,000 | (8.3) | 300,000 | (14.9) | 290,000 | 200,000(19.5)(22.7) | 1,678.93"
      },
      {
        "passage_id": "P2",
        "quote": "It appears that delays in production and shipping are gradually recovering."
      },
      {
        "passage_id": "P3",
        "quote": "the\ncombination of existing popular titles and a continuous stream of appealing new titles will work together to invigorate the platform."
      }
    ],
    "calculation_ids": []
  },
  "alternative_and_signal": {
    "text": "Downside alternative: persistent component shortages could cost Nintendo console sales and the software purchases those new owners would otherwise make during the year. I would switch to a lower call if Nintendo cut its full-year Switch unit forecast and attributed the cut to parts shortages. That would indicate the delays were reducing expected annual volume, not just shifting shipments between quarters (PDF pp. 3–4).",
    "citations": [
      {
        "passage_id": "P2",
        "quote": "However, we may be affected if there continue to be\nissues involving the procurement of necessary components."
      },
      {
        "passage_id": "P3",
        "quote": "Unit sales of key products used in these forecasts can be found on page 14"
      }
    ],
    "calculation_ids": []
  },
  "direction": "within"
}

